Please evaluate the following ID design as a senior backend/database architect.

Context:
I am designing IDs for a large distributed system with microservices, high concurrency, DB/table sharding, long-term evolvability, and operational observability.

My goals:
- good insert/query/index performance
- global uniqueness
- support for distributed ID generation without single point of failure
- support for DB/table sharding
- time ordering as much as possible
- stable ID format across future sharding evolution
- better troubleshooting from logs when only an ID is available
- separation between internal technical ID and external business-facing ID

Design:

1. PK id: InnoDB auto-increment bigint
Reason:
a. more efficient insertion, fewer disk fragments, better locality, fewer page splits
b. bigint comparison is more efficient than varchar
c. smaller secondary index overhead than string PK

2. internal-id: string, globally unique, supports db/table sharding, time ordering, and no single point of failure

Format:
(env-state) + (sub-system-id) + (business-type-id) + (virtual-route-bucket-number) + (unique-id-generated-from-id-generator)

The generated unique ID is a long value composed from:
(milliseconds-timestamp) + (region/AZ/IDC) + (local node group) + (worker-seq-in-group) + (sequence within millisecond)

Example meaning:
- 1 = production env
- 001 = payment service
- 01 = payment order
- body = generated unique number

Example internal-id:
1001017263819283719283

Reason:
a. I want a uniform internal-id format across a large system
b. adding env-state + subsystem-id + business-type-id helps troubleshooting when logs are incomplete and I only have the ID
c. env-state is reference information only, not the final truth, but it should normally match real environment
d. virtual-route-bucket-number is used because real sharding count/strategy may evolve, while internal-id should stay as stable as possible
e. sometimes sharding depends on external/business grouping; for example, all wallet-actions of one wallet-txn should land in the same shard/table, so I may need shard routing info from the action-id itself
f. I separate generated-unique-id from business metadata because the generator should only focus on uniqueness, while business logic wraps it with readable/routable metadata
g. the generated unique ID is compact as long; wrapped internal-id is string for readability and metadata

3. external-biz-id: string with readable prefix, unique per subsystem, hides underlying design, shorter, stable across business evolution
Example:
ORD-20260417-<base62(longId)>

